A||||6||GarethWright||Wiring for Remote Trunk Release||||1||
Z||000000||GarethWright||03-20-2001||01:41 AM||||Hello all.<P>I am trying to wire up a remote trunk release.  <BR>What is the wire leading into the electric trunk release providing, +12v? if so, how is it grounded? <BR> <BR>The alarm I am using works on the principle that the release solenoid gets +12v but no ground, so the trunk release switch completes the ground circuit.  The alarm splices into the ground wire for the trunk release and hits it with 200mA neg. ground when triggered. <BR>On the back of the Trunk release switch I see an orange/black stripe, Gray/black stripe, and 2  light gray wires.  Am I right in thinking that the orange/black and gray/black are power to the light in the switch?  Which wire at the trunk release switch do I need to splice the alarm wire into, one of the light gray ones? <BR>Thanks,<BR>Gareth<P>ps If this works, the next project is remote start, so keep those wiring diagrams handy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||DRH||03-20-2001||02:26 AM||||This is for an 88... I assume the others are the same???<P>The switch is providing +12. The solenoid  is grounded in thru the trunk wiring.<P>Light Gray is + for the light<BR>Black is - for the light<BR>Orange/Dark Blue is power from the 25 Amp Ctsy/Lid Fuse<BR>Gray/Black runs to the trunk release relay<P>The trunk release relay is a little silver box under the right side of the steering column. It's a safety relay to make sure an Auto is in Park/Neutral or a manual has the Parking Brake on.<P>Sounds like you are going to need a relay that connects Orange/Dark Blue to Gray/Black when it's energized. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||862M4inCA||03-20-2001||03:17 AM||||Just a thought but wouldn't it be easier, and safer if the solenoid is only energized by a +12 signal, to make the connection somewhere between the switch and the trunk relay? If so I'm sure your alarm has a aux. positive feed that you can use....maybe one for a remote start or power door lock? As I remember when installing my alarm the only wire that allowed for a negative connection aside from ground was for the door pin switches for certain makes and models.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||03-20-2001||10:42 AM||||I am surprised that your alarm did not say to use a relay, usually most alarms dont put out enough current to fire things like door locks and trunks.  Anyway, here is what I did for my alarm:<P>get a 30amp automotive grade relay, cost about $1.00 (I got a bunch of mine from <A HREF="http://www.partsexpress.com)" TARGET=_blank>www.partsexpress.com)</A> <P>run a splice from the gray/black and orange/dark blue wire<P>connect the gray/black splice to terminal 87 of the relay.  connect the orange/dark blue splice to terminal 30<P>connect terminal 85 to +12 that is hot at all times (you can use the orange/dark blue wire for this)<P>connect terminal 86 to the ground that is provided by your alarm.<P>So, when hit your trunk pop button, the alarm gives the coil in the relay a connection to ground, and because it already has + voltage, the coil throws a switch inside of it to connect the 30 and 87 terminals.  So, when these are connected, +12v is sent to the soleniod.  The soleniod grounds out through the metal hook that the trunk latch catches on.<P>In essence, you are just adding another switch to do the same thing as the regular (dash) switch.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||GarethWright||03-20-2001||07:52 PM||||Oops, I am supposed to use a relay, but forgot that part  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>FieroJoe, Thanks that looks like it will work, theres a (poor)wiring diagram with the instructions, but I wasn't sure which wires do what up there. <BR>Thanks guys<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||03-20-2001||07:52 PM||||The Fiero power trunk latch DOES NOT ground thru the wiring. It grounds to the latch strike. The 3 wires for the ajar/light switch have no electrical connection to the latch.||||reg||1
Z||000006||GarethWright||03-21-2001||12:43 AM||||Hello, Well I got that working, now onto the remote start  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Thanks for all the great info and help guys.<BR>Gareth (who can now open his trunk from a distance  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)||||reg||1
